  {
   "id": "contain",
   "type": "decision",
   "prompt": "It's 17:25 UTC. What do you recommend?",
   "context": "What you know: one desktop is infected; files on the main file share are encrypted; the infection came from a USB stick; nothing yet shows other machines infected. Last night's backup of the file server exists, but nobody can say when a restore was last tested.",
   "options": [
    {
     "id": "restore",
     "text": "Disconnect the desktop and the file server from the network now, then restore the file server from last night's backup.",
     "feedback": "This stops any further encryption and gets staff working soonest, if the backup is recent, complete and not encrypted too. Has anyone tested a restore? (Recall S13's triage scenario 6.) Restoring before you know how the malware arrived risks reinfection when the USB stick comes back."
    },
    {
     "id": "readonly",
     "text": "Isolate the desktop only. Keep the file server online but read-only while you work out which files were hit.",
     "feedback": "This keeps the business running on the files that survived and causes the least disruption. It's risky if anything else is infected, because the server stays reachable. You need to be confident the desktop was the only source of encryption."
    },
    {
     "id": "shutdown",
     "text": "Shut down every Windows machine on the network until you've confirmed nothing else is infected.",
     "feedback": "This is maximum containment, and very defensible while you don't know the scope. But it halts the whole business, and powering machines off destroys memory evidence. The CFO will want to know how long, and you won't be able to say."
    },
    {
     "id": "pay",
     "text": "Isolate the desktop and pay the ransom to get the decryption key quickly.",
     "feedback": "Some organisations do pay, usually on legal and insurer advice. Payment funds criminals, can be illegal if the group is sanctioned, doesn't guarantee a working key, and marks you as someone who pays. With working backups it's rarely necessary, and it's a decision for executives and counsel, not the SOC."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "soar",
   "type": "reflection",
   "prompt": "Looking ahead to next class: which of these steps should a playbook do automatically, without waiting for a person? Tick all that apply.",
   "options": [
    {
     "id": "case",
     "text": "Open a high-priority case when an IDS signature names a ransomware family",
     "feedback": "A good automation candidate: fast and low-risk, and a person still decides what to do."
    },
    {
     "id": "enrich",
     "text": "Look up the infected machine's owner and asset details",
     "feedback": "Enrichment is the classic SOAR task: pure lookups, no risk."
    },
    {
     "id": "isolate",
     "text": "Cut the infected desktop off the network",
     "feedback": "Often automated for confirmed ransomware, because minutes matter. The risk is that a false positive takes someone's machine offline. Many teams automate this for workstations but require approval for servers."
    },
    {
     "id": "disable",
     "text": "Disable the user's account",
     "feedback": "Usually needs a person. It locks someone out of their work, and this user was a victim, not the attacker."
    },
    {
     "id": "restore",
     "text": "Restore the file server from backup",
     "feedback": "Keep a person in the loop. A restore overwrites data and must wait until the infection is contained."
    },
    {
     "id": "ransom",
     "text": "Decide whether to pay",
     "feedback": "Never automated. It's a decision for executives, legal and the insurer."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
